Operational Support Guide – Spring Boot Microservices Platform
1. Purpose & Scope

This document serves as the official operational reference for supporting and maintaining the Spring Boot microservices platform. It is intended for the L2/L3 Support Team, Infrastructure Team, and Audit Stakeholders.
The scope covers deployment, monitoring, logging, troubleshooting, and incident handling for all microservices running in UAT and Production environments.

2. System Overview

The platform consists of multiple Spring Boot services:

CTX Service –  processing

STX Service – processing

INY Service – Read-only inquiry API

SWH Service – End-of-day (EOD) toggle process

All services are hosted on Windows Servers (except SWH which runs as a scheduled command-line process triggered by SQL job).

Backend database: SQL Server

Caching: Elastic Cache for inquiry optimization

Logging & Monitoring: ELK Stack (Filebeat → Logstash → Elastic Cloud → Kibana)

Security & Access Control: Active Directory (AD) based authentication + PAM-controlled Prod access

3. Deployment Process
3.1 UAT Deployment

Build artifacts using Maven.

Package service ZIP (JAR + dependencies + config).

Copy ZIP to UAT server.

Extract and configure environment-specific properties (application-uat.properties).

Register as Windows Service via WinSW.

Validate service startup and log availability.

3.2 Production Deployment

Deployment is performed through PAM (Privileged Access Management).

Copy packaged artifact to Prod server.

Update application-prod.properties (kept outside JAR for flexibility).

Start service via Windows Services console.

Verify logs in service.log and confirm connectivity with SQL DB.

Validate end-to-end transaction flow via test SOAP/REST API calls.

4. Configuration Management

Config files are externalized for each environment:

application-uat.properties

application-prod.properties

Sensitive values (DB passwords, service accounts) are vaulted in PAM and injected during deployment.

Services read environment-specific configs at startup (Spring Profiles).

5. Monitoring & Logging
5.1 Logs

Location: <service_home>/logs/

Files:

application.log → system events, warnings, errors

service.log → SOAP/REST request/response details

Log rotation is configured for 30 days.

5.2 ELK Integration

Filebeat ships logs from servers to Logstash.

Logstash processes and enriches log data.

Logs stored in Elastic Cloud.

Kibana dashboards available for transaction status, errors, and performance metrics.

5.3 Monitoring

Spring Boot Actuator provides health endpoints:

/actuator/health

/actuator/metrics

Alerts configured in ELK (Watcher rules for failure spikes).

6. Operational Procedures

Start/Stop Services: via Windows Service Manager or command line.

Check Logs: open service.log or use Kibana.

Cache Refresh: automated daily job; manual refresh triggered via API if required.

Database Failover: switch between primary and secondary DB handled by config.

Service Restart Policy: all services auto-restart on failure (WinSW config).

7. Incident Management

Detection: Alerts via Kibana/ELK or user reports.

Initial Triage:

Check service status

Review logs (service.log)

Validate DB connectivity

Resolution: Restart service or escalate.

Escalation Matrix:

L1 Support: Basic triage, restart services

L2 Support: Deeper investigation, log analysis, cache troubleshooting

L3/Engineering: Code-level issues, DB schema changes, bug fixes

8. Change Management

All deployments require ABC approval.

Changes logged in Confluence Release Notes.

Rollback Plan: revert to previous JAR + config.

9. Security & Access Control

All services run under Windows Service Accounts.

Authenticated against Active Directory (AD).

PAM controls Prod access (engineers use temporary credentials).

Audit logs capture:

Deployment actions

Service restarts

Config changes

10. Roadmap Alignment

Current process: manual Maven builds and deployments via PAM.

Future state:

DevOps Pipeline (Jenkins/GitLab CI)

Containerization (Docker)

Orchestration (OpenShift/Kubernetes)

Migration work is in progress and tracked in the Tech Roadmap.
